  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Project Requirements**\n\nOkay, I\'m meticulously breaking down each user requirement, mapping it directly onto our existing codebase. I\'m focusing on how React, TypeScript, Vite, and Tailwind CSS v4 can address each element. The design constitution is key to ensuring visual and functional alignment. I\'m building a project analysis & architectural plan.\n\n\n**Defining Brand Identity**\n\nI\'ve selected \"AuraSafe\" as the application name; it resonates well. My tagline is now \"Personal safety, guardian connection, and rapid emergency readiness.\" The palette is firmed up: primary rich purple, canvas white and soft alabaster, structural surfaces of soft lavender, and red for emergency accents. Plus Jakarta Sans, DM Sans, and JetBrains Mono are the typeface choices.\n\n\n**Refining UI Component Design**\n\nMy focus is sharpening on user interface elements. I\'m eliminating static pill designs; metadata will be conveyed with text separators or interactive elements. No pulsing dots or bold left borders for status indicators. It\'s mobile-first with a bottom navigation, but desktop users will see a fully responsive, 1440px layout with a dashboard grid. My goal is a consistent and intuitive experience across devices.\n\n\n**Prioritizing User Clarity**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on UI text, aiming for extreme clarity. Button labels like \"Sign In\", \"Start Travel\", and \"Send SOS\" will guide users intuitively. I\'m also ensuring prototype transparency, clearly labeling demo flows for OTP, AI Assistant, and integrations. Every button in these demos will function as designed in the browser. My focus remains on straightforward, human-centered language.\n\n\n**Implementing User Authentication**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the sign-in flow. Password visibility toggles, and clear error messages are critical. The forgot password flow will be a modal, allowing local password resets, while using the local storage for the demo. I am using local storage to both store the user accounts for registration and current sessions, and ensuring returning users can sign in easily without re-registration, or sign in through a demo account button.\n\n\n**Designing Account Flow**\n\nMy recent focus is on the account creation and sign-in flow. Users can sign in with name or phone number, along with their password, after registering. There\'s a demo account option (Maya Sharma / 9876543210 / Safe@1234) for quick testing, and a Google Sign-In with a clear disclaimer. Screen 2 is for registration with required fields. Validation is being implemented across all fields.\n\n\n**Developing OTP Verification**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the demo OTP implementation. I\'m building independent verification sections for both user and guardian phone numbers. Each includes a dedicated \"Send OTP\" button, a simulated 6-digit code display, and clearly labelled demo messages. Account creation will only be enabled after form validity checks and successful completion of both OTP verifications. I\'m also ensuring all actions have appropriate error feedback.\n\n\n**Constructing Dashboard Header**\n\nI\'m now implementing the Home Dashboard header elements. The AuraSafe logo is set, and a personalized welcome message is in place. Notifications, Profile, and Settings icons are functioning, opening their respective drawers/modals. On mobile, the header compacts for space, while the bottom navigation keeps the experience consistent. The key next step is the SOS Emergency Card/Control.\n\n\n**Defining SOS Emergency Flow**\n\nI\'m now implementing the SOS Emergency Confirmation Modal. This includes a clear Ready/Alert state, displays guardian and saved contacts, and integrates `navigator.geolocation` for location, with graceful handling if denied. I\'ve included `tel:` links for emergency calls and a demonstrative alert dispatch to the recipient list. I\'m ensuring quick access to Manage SOS Contacts from both the SOS modal and the dashboard/settings.\n\n\n**Implementing Travel Mode**\n\nI am now fully engaged in building the Travel Mode, starting with a UI card for journey setup, then opening a modal to configure the journey parameters. I\'m focusing on user-friendly toggles for starting and ending travel, input fields for locations, guardian contact selection from user\'s contacts. The Travel Status indicator and journey timer are under construction, with a focus on an intuitive user interface.\n\n\n**Developing Safety Feature Integration**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing interactive safety elements for the dashboard. This includes optional attention sirens, immediate mute/stop functionalities, and an SOS confirmation flow. My plan incorporates an SOS contacts manager, enabling the addition, editing, and deletion of emergency contacts. I\'m also implementing smart safety concepts like shake detection and voice activation with clear, browser-aware disclaimers. I want to build a test for the 3-shake gesture.\n\n\n**Integrating Voice Control**\n\nI\'m now integrating voice control features and incorporating emergency keyword detection, which will trigger the SOS flow. I\'m focusing on microphone permission indicators, browser speech API support detection, and interactive buttons to start and stop listening. I\'m also including a test button for keyword matching. Importantly, I\'m providing clear disclaimers regarding browser limitations for continuous background listening.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'m starting camera gesture integration. I\'ve built a live video preview using `getUserMedia`, and adding start/stop control, with handling for unsupported and denied camera permissions, and adding a gesture recognition overlay.\n\n\n**Adding Location Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating nearby location features into the home dashboard. I\'ve included controls for finding nearby police stations and restrooms using geolocation. I am working on the map integration placeholder, and ensuring I clearly explain that live nearby search requires a Maps/Places API integration. I\'ve also incorporated working directions/search links.\n\n\n**Refining Location Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating nearby location features into the home dashboard. I\'ve included controls for finding nearby police stations and restrooms using geolocation. I am working on the map integration placeholder, and ensuring I clearly explain that live nearby search requires a Maps/Places API integration. I\'ve also incorporated working directions/search links. I\'m focusing on showing clearly structured location card schema previews, marked as requiring a Maps API connection. I\'ll include real emergency directory numbers too.\n\n\n**Constructing Helpful Chatbot**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in building a supportive chat feature to promote user safety. I\'m focusing on user\'s name message tailoring and quick safety question buttons. I\'ve built a chat input and scrollable message thread. I am building a smart local rule-based response engine. I will provide safety tips, app features, and emergency numbers.\n\n\n**Designing Profile/Settings Sections**\n\nI\'m now designing the user Profile and Settings sections as modal drawers. The Profile section will have a photo placeholder and display user data, with an inline edit mode that updates `localStorage`. The Settings section will house password change functionality, a link to the SOS contact manager, and notification preferences. The implementation is ongoing!\n\n\n**Implementing Logout and Settings**\n\nI\'m now implementing interactive toggles for travel reminders, alerts, guardian updates, and alarm chimes, all persisting in local storage. Privacy settings toggles, covering GPS sharing, notification masking, and local data reset are also being developed. Smart trigger shortcuts (Shake, Voice, Gesture demos) are in progress. A logout button, clearing the active session while retaining user profiles, is also in progress. I\'m focusing on user-centered design.\n\n\n"
