  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining The Approach**\n\nI\'m now thinking about making `/api/threat/nearby-police` super reliable. I\'ve got a 3-tier system in mind. Tier 1 will be `gemini-4-hydrogen` with Google Maps tools for official grounding and map URIs, assuming quota\'s available. Tier 2 is up next.\n\n\n**Implementing Fallbacks**\n\nI\'ve been working on fallback strategies for the police station lookup API. If Gemini encounters errors or rate limits, the system now automatically shifts to Tier 2: using Google Search grounding with Gemini to find official police station details. If that fails, Tier 3 kicks in, using OpenStreetMap Nominatim for coordinates, addresses, and Google Maps links. The integration of OpenStreetMap allows for reverse geocoding of GPS coordinates to pinpoint the city/neighborhood for more accurate Google Search results.\n\n\n**Addressing Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on eliminating 429 errors from the `/api/threat/nearby-police` endpoint in Threat Mode. I\'m implementing a multi-tiered approach to ensure a reliable response. First, the `/server.ts` file needs to be updated to prevent raw JSON dumps from being shown to users. I\'m working to make sure the error handling is more user-friendly, with a clean dismiss button on the page. Specifically, I\'m targeting the `nearbyPoliceError` variable in `ThreatModePage.tsx` to ensure it always displays a human-readable error, never raw JSON.\n\n\n"
