  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Prompt\'s Core**\n\nI\'m now carefully unpacking this Tamil request. The user needs an SOS call accessible without app entry. The current design demands app interaction, which is a blocker. My focus is understanding the precise needs in translation, and the context of an app-based emergency.\n\n\n**Expanding Feature Set**\n\nI\'ve got three trigger options for SOS: shake, voice code, and tap. My core goal is to enable an SOS call without app navigation, addressing the emergency-first purpose. The current app\'s SOS system is not truly an SOS system if one needs to open the app, as then it is no better than calling. I\'m prioritizing direct-activation features, and have thought of the React Web/PWA implementation in relation to native OS boundaries.\n\n\n**Assessing Feature Fit**\n\nMy focus is now on skill applicability. Frontend design seems relevant only for minor UI additions to the existing OurSafe style, so I\'ll check `pwa-integration`. The user\'s focus on background triggering (shake, voice, tap) aligns perfectly with PWA capabilities: installability, manifest, service workers, and relevant web APIs like `DeviceMotionEvent`, `SpeechRecognition`, and `pointerdown` to enable rapid triggers on screen, along with Android/iOS Companion and Lock-Screen widget feasibility.\n\n\n**Inspecting Code and Purpose**\n\nI\'m now diving into the code. I\'ll analyze existing files like `HomeDashboard.tsx`, `SettingsModal.tsx`, and relevant components. Simultaneously, I\'ll ensure the `/api/threat/nearby-police` endpoint functions correctly. My primary goal is to understand the current implementation before considering changes, especially regarding the emergency trigger options and their integration. I need to be sure before I modify anything.\n\n\n**Evaluating PWA Potential**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on PWA integration. While the user didn\'t explicitly mention \"PWA,\" their need for SOS activation \"without going inside the app\" is the key. The challenge is the OS\'s security sandbox preventing direct hardware interception when the browser is completely closed. But, when minimized, in standby shield mode, or on any screen, the three triggers (shake, voice, and tap) work live using real web APIs. Specifically, the triple-shake trigger detects real accelerometer jerks, supporting iOS 13+ permission requests and offering a desktop shake test. The secret code voice trigger functions like \"Hey Google\".\n\n\n**Implementing Trigger Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific trigger implementations. `SpeechRecognition` is the key for the custom secret code SOS trigger, even when the screen is locked. I\'m exploring the `Wake Lock API` to prevent the screen from turning off, ensuring continuous microphone and sensor access. I am also working on global screen tap detection, allowing users to choose between double or triple taps for a rapid SOS trigger, even in standby mode. This should initiate an SOS countdown or instant call depending on settings.\n\n\n"
