  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining Code Structures**\n\nI\'ve been reviewing the code related to safety features, specifically focusing on `storage.ts` and `types/safety.ts`. I\'m comparing the stored safety preferences, like shake detection, voice activation, and voice keyword, with their implementations, which seems promising. I\'m noting specific lines, aiming to determine best approach.\n\n\n**Analyzing User Requirements**\n\nI\'ve discovered the root cause of the user\'s issue: previously enabled safety features are now disabled by default. The user desires direct SOS triggers\342\200\224shake, voice wake-word, and screen taps\342\200\224without app access. These were previously in a section of the code that was removed and the preferences stored the features as false. This clarifies the discrepancy.\n\n\n**Designing Emergency Trigger Engine**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in designing the emergency trigger engine. The goal is a seamless, functional system across the app, including lock-screen functionality on phones and laptops. I\'m updating `SafetyPreferences` in `/src/types/safety.ts` and `DEFAULT_PREFERENCES` in `/src/utils/storage.ts` to include `shakeDetectionEnabled`, `voiceActivationEnabled`, and other crucial settings with smart defaults.\n\n\n**Defining Trigger Functionality**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the precise behavior of the emergency triggers. I\'m focusing on features such as a secret voice code, screen tap triggers, and automatic dialing, configurable in the settings. I\'m updating `SafetyPreferences` to include settings like secret voice codes, double-tap vs triple-tap, and countdown options. I\'m also considering countdown delays with an option for an instant call.\n\n\n**Implementing Shake Detection**\n\nI\'m deep in the implementation of the shake detection feature. I\'m focusing on the `devicemotion` event listener and refining the shake impulse detection using accelerometer data. I\'ve defined a clear threshold and debounce mechanism to filter out false positives and track consecutive shakes within a sliding window. There\'s also a live shake count with visual feedback, and I\'ve ensured compatibility with iOS Safari\'s permission requirements, including a helpful button. I\'ve built a simulator for easy desktop testing, replicating the exact same state transitions.\n\n\n**Developing Voice Activation**\n\nI\'m now focused on the voice activation aspect. I\'m implementing a system that listens for a secret code, which includes fuzzy matching of spoken transcripts against a configured secret code. I\'ve designed an indicator to show a live transcript while the mic is active, automatically deactivating when the mic is off or in use by another feature to avoid interference. I\'m also ensuring the functionality has testability for users on devices without a mic, so users can verify the behavior right from the trigger panel.\n\n\n**Implementing Screen Tap Trigger**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing the screen tap trigger. I\'ve chosen `pointerdown` to listen globally, supporting both mobile and desktop input. I\'m focusing on double or triple taps within 650ms. I\'m building smart anti-accidental guards. Outside normal dashboard screens, inputs, text areas, and buttons are ignored, preventing false alarms. The lock screen will register any tap. Finally, I\'m including visual and haptic feedback.\n\n\n**Designing Lock-Screen Shield**\n\nI\'m now designing the Lock-Screen Shield, solving the core user issue. This is now a fully usable demo accessible from several key areas. I am implementing the Screen Wake Lock API to keep the screen active. I\'m focusing on a minimal, dark, OLED-friendly interface, complete with clock, date, and status indicators. This will provide a reliable standby mode for emergency triggers.\n\n\n**Constructing User Interface**\n\nI\'m now building the user interface for the Lock-Screen Shield. It features three armed triggers: Shake, Voice, and Tap. A direct touch zone on the dark lock screen triggers an instant SOS call, and there\'s a live interactive test bar at the bottom, offering real touch/click testing for all triggers on laptops or phones. I\'ve designed a slide/button to exit the shield and enter the app.\n\nI\'m working on the `EmergencyTriggerOverlay.tsx` component, which will manage the immediate response when any of the three triggers activates.\n\n\n**Defining Emergency Overlay**\n\nI\'m now designing the Emergency SOS Dispatch Overlay. This high-contrast, full-screen overlay will display the specific trigger used. A countdown, with cancel and immediate call options, will be shown. At the end of the countdown, it will record the SOS event, get the live GPS, and trigger an SOS alert to guardians. It will then make the emergency call, and present buttons to call guardian or police. I\'m also including haptic vibration and an alert tone.\n\n\n**Implementing SOS Panel**\n\nI\'m now building the Quick SOS Triggers panel. I\'m focusing on a structured design within `SosPage.tsx` that also incorporates functionality within `HomeDashboard.tsx` and `SettingsModal.tsx`. I\'m prioritizing clear and customizable options for users. This includes a \"Hands-Free & Outside-App Emergency Triggers\" card with toggleable shake, voice, and tap triggers. I\'m adding a live sensor status and permission button for shake detection. Additionally, I\'m integrating a secret code input for voice activation with language selection.\n\n\n"
