  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Code Structure**\n\nI\'m impressed by the codebase\'s clarity; it\'s a great foundation. I\'m focusing on making my implementation clean, modular, and fully functional, targeting specific files. I will update `/src/types/safety.ts` by extending `SafetyPreferences`.\n\n\n**Defining Quick Trigger Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating new features for quick SOS triggers. I\'m focusing on defining `QuickTriggerKind` and `QuickTriggerEvent` types. I have a list of new preferences, including `shakeCountRequired`, `voiceCodeLang`, `screenTapSosEnabled`, `screenTapCount`, `autoDialOnTrigger`, and `quickTriggerCountdownSec`. I am looking to set the defaults in `DEFAULT_PREFERENCES` within the `/src/utils/storage.ts` file.\n\n\n**Implementing Emergency Triggers**\n\nI\'m finalizing the logic for emergency triggers. I\'ve set `autoDialOnTrigger` to true and `quickTriggerCountdownSec` to 3. I\'m adding `getPrimaryEmergencyUser()` to handle emergency contacts, even before login, and ensuring user data upgrades handle previous settings gracefully, defaulting new fields to enabled if the user has not explicitly disabled them. I\'ll make the updates in the file `/src/utils/storage.ts`.\n\n\n**Refining Shake and Voice Triggers**\n\nI\'ve been working on a new React hook in `/src/hooks/useEmergencyQuickTriggers.ts` to unify the three trigger types. I\'ve got the shake trigger working with accelerometer delta detection and proper debouncing, including iOS permission handling. The voice wake-word trigger is set up for continuous listening and supports English and Tamil, normalizing transcripts for accurate matching.\n\n\n**Developing Voice & Tap Triggers**\n\nI\'m now integrating the voice and screen-tap triggers, and they\'re progressing smoothly. I\'ve implemented a cooldown to prevent accidental re-triggers of the voice trigger, and the `testSecretCodeMatch()` method enables easy testing. The screen-tap trigger is a global listener on the window that supports touch screen and mouse click, with timing and radius constraints for accuracy. I\'ve also incorporated the `isLockScreenMode` check, and utility.\n\n\n**Implementing Tap Detection**\n\nI\'m now focusing on screen tap detection. I\'ve excluded interactive elements from triggering SOS when the app is in normal usage. I\'m tracking `tapProgress` with a reset after 700ms, and playing haptic feedback for each tap/shake and emergency patterns. The new overlay in `/src/components/EmergencyTriggerOverlay.tsx` displays the trigger details, regardless of how SOS is triggered.\n\n\n**Finalizing Trigger Dispatch**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final stage for trigger dispatch. I\'ve integrated a countdown with prominent \"CALL NOW\" and \"Cancel\" buttons, skipping to the call if `quickTriggerCountdownSec` is zero. A confirmation beep is played using Web Audio, circumventing autoplay restrictions. On dispatch, the `onDispatchConfirmed(event)` function is called, logging SOS, obtaining GPS data, alerting guardians, and enabling Dual Call state. If `autoDialOnTrigger` is true, the system attempts to dial the guardian directly, with fallback `tel:` links. Finally, I\'m displaying large, high-contrast buttons for calling the guardian or police.\n\n\n**Designing Lock Screen Shield**\n\nI\'m now building the Emergency Lock Screen Shield in `/src/components/EmergencyLockScreenShield.tsx`. The goal is an \"Outside-App SOS Call\" \342\200\224 triggering SOS without launching the app, ideal for lock screen operation. It requests a `screen` wake lock to keep the screen alive, enabling all triggers. The UI mirrors a dark lock screen, featuring a subtle lock icon, network status, digital time, date, and live tap/shake feedback visuals. It will include **SEND EMERGENCY GPS SMS (`sms:...`)** option, as well as an option to **Open Full SOS Page** or to **Dismiss**.\n\n\n**Developing Trigger Status Tiles**\n\nI\'m now building a compact tray with armed trigger status. There will be three tiles. The first is for the shake trigger, with live dot progress and a test button. Next is for the voice trigger, displaying the current transcript and a microphone toggle with a quick test button. The final tile is for tap, showing the required tap count with live progress.\n\n\n**Integrating Trigger Tiles**\n\nI\'m now integrating the trigger tiles into the app interface. I\'m focusing on the `SettingsModal.tsx` file to place the components. I\'m building out a settings panel with separate sections for shake, voice, and tap triggers with their options. I\'m wiring this into the main app components such as `App.tsx`, `LoginScreen.tsx`, and `HomeDashboard.tsx` for access across the user experience. I\'m also ensuring the \"Lock-Screen Shield\" CTA is prominent.\n\n\n**Refactoring Trigger Components**\n\nI\'m now refactoring trigger components for improved integration and functionality. I\'m focusing on unifying the logic to enable emergency triggers both logged-in and logged-out, using `useEmergencyQuickTriggers` in `HomeDashboard` and `App.tsx` respectively. I\'m adding a \"Lock Shield\" button to `HomeDashboard` for direct access to the lock-screen emergency mode.\n\n\n"
