  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Core Feature Specs**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the requested features, starting with the triple phone shake SOS. I\'m focusing on validating its practical implementation without requiring the app to be open. Ensuring reliability and minimal false triggers are key. I\'m prioritizing understanding how the shake detection will function and interface with the phone\'s system.\n\n\n**Analyzing New Functionality**\n\nI\'m now expanding my focus to include voice activation and screen tap functionalities. Specifically, I\'m examining continuous `SpeechRecognition` to enable a \"Hey Google\"-style wake-word, ensuring it can function with custom codes and diverse language support. I\'m also planning the rapid screen tap detector and lock-screen options. This is a big step to consider global responsiveness.\n\n\n**Implementing Lock Screen Functionality**\n\nI\'m now integrating a live, dark OLED-style lock screen utilizing the `wakeLock` API. This will keep the three triggers\342\200\224triple shake, secret voice code, and double/triple screen tap\342\200\224active outside the app dashboard. I\'m also planning a test drawer for direct trigger validation. The goal is seamless SOS activation and a quick emergency call, triggered instantly, even from the login screen or lock-screen.\n\n\n**Designing Quick SOS Controls**\n\nI\'m now designing the control panel. I\'m focusing on the `QuickSosTriggersCard.tsx` component, ensuring it\'s prominent on the SOS page and mirrored in the SettingsModal. I\'ll enable all three options by default. The plan includes live status meters, an editable secret code, a 2x vs 3x tap toggle, a countdown setting, an always-on mic toggle, and interactive demo controls for each trigger.\n\n\n**Finalizing Trigger Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the core functionality. I\'m focusing on a helper function, `getEmergencyFallbackUser()`, to retrieve emergency contact details from the login screen, even before login, which is critical. I\'ve set up three triggers: triple shake, voice code with English and Tamil support, and double/triple tap. I\'m also implementing the lock screen overlay to enable these features.\n\n\n**Integrating Global Trigger Access**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating the Emergency SOS features across the app. This involves the overlay, lock screen shield, and control panel. I\'m prioritizing integration within `App.tsx` and the `LoginScreen.tsx` to ensure triggers work before signing in. My goals include adding a \"Lock-Screen SOS Shield\" button to the login screen and an armed-triggers status bar to ensure the user never has to sign in during an emergency.\n\n\n"
