  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering User Preferences**\n\nI\'m focusing on ensuring older user preferences, specifically related to shake detection and voice activation, are handled correctly when retrieving from local storage in `storage.ts`. I need to ensure that users whose initial preferences were set to disabled are not stuck with those settings if they\'re now defaulted to enabled. This is important for a smooth user experience.\n\n\n**Refining Migration Logic**\n\nI\'ve decided to refine the migration strategy. Instead of directly altering the preference retrieval in `hydrateUser`, I\'ll focus on a one-time migration check. This will merge missing fields from `DEFAULT_PREFERENCES`, which includes the new trigger settings. Specifically, I\'ll check for the existence of new fields like `screenTapSosEnabled`, and if they\'re missing *and* shake or voice were previously disabled, I will default the new trigger settings to `true` on upgrade. This addresses the potential issue of stale local storage blocking the fix. I\'ll update `hydrateUser` now.\n\n\n**Designing New Components**\n\nI\'m now fully engaged in the design phase for the three new components. I\'m carefully outlining the visual and functional aspects of `/src/components/EmergencyTriggerOverlay.tsx`, focusing on its role as the full-screen urgent overlay. I\'m detailing the displayed information, including trigger source badges, countdown logic with cancel and call options, and the automatic dispatch actions involving `onDispatchSos(event)`. I am also working on `EmergencyLockScreenShield.tsx` and `QuickSosTriggersCard.tsx` in parallel.\n\n\n**Developing Emergency Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the visual and interactive design of the emergency call cards within `EmergencyTriggerOverlay.tsx`. I\'m prioritizing clear visual hierarchy with prominent \"CALL GUARDIAN,\" \"CALL POLICE,\" and \"SEND LIVE GPS SMS\" options, each including relevant information. I\'m also including a button to \"Open Full SOS Dashboard\" and \"Dismiss & Mark Safe.\" Furthermore, I\'ve designed a full-screen, dark, lock-screen shield component, `EmergencyLockScreenShield.tsx`, providing persistent trigger access. It is optimized for OLED displays, includes a status bar, a central live clock, and tap/shake progress indicators.\n\n\n**Constructing Lock-Screen Interface**\n\nI\'m deep in the implementation of the lock screen elements. I\'m building a live status strip with indicators for shake, voice, and screen tap triggers. It includes interactive quick test buttons for each trigger: \"Shake,\" \"Mic ON/OFF,\" and \"Tap Test.\" The screen features a prominent \"Unlock & Return to App\" button, marked to prevent accidental SOS activation. I\'m also ensuring the component maintains screen wake lock while active.\n\n\n**Implementing Test Functionality**\n\nI\'m now implementing interactive test buttons within the lock screen components. I\'ve added a button that will allow users to test the outside-app lock screen in a single click. I\'m also developing three side-by-side interactive cards for shake, and secret voice code triggers, structured for clarity and easy testing. The shake trigger card includes an on/off switch, live shake counter with visual dots, and an iOS Motion Permission button (if needed). I\'m also implementing an interactive \"Test Shake\" button to verify the SOS sequence. For the secret voice code trigger card, I\'m integrating an on/off switch, editable secret code input with quick preset chips, a language selector, a \"Start Always-On Voice Listener\" button with live word display and a \"Test Secret Code\" button.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "euACa5KuFI7Uz7IPvOTf6QQ"
    tool_calls {
