  planner_response {
    response: "**Honest answer: Right now, it works only inside the app (as an in-app Lock-Screen Standby mode), NOT on your phone\'s real system lock screen.**\n\n### Why?\nBecause this version is a **Web Application (running in a browser like Chrome/Safari)**. Both **Android and iOS strictly block websites** from reading the accelerometer (shake), listening to the microphone (voice code), detecting screen taps, or placing automatic phone calls once you press the phone\'s power button to lock the device or close the browser.\n\n### What works right now in this Web App\n- When you tap **Lock Shield**, OurSafe uses the browser\'s **Screen Wake Lock** to keep the screen from sleeping and displays a dark lock-screen clock cover.\n- As long as that shield screen is open, **3x Shake**, **Secret Voice Code**, and **Double/Triple Screen Tap** all work live without navigating inside the app dashboard.\n\n### What is needed for the *real* total phone lock screen (when phone is locked / app closed)\nTo work on the actual OS lock screen (like **\"Hey Google\"** or Android\'s built-in Emergency SOS), this frontend needs to be wrapped or built as a **Native Android/iOS app** (for example, wrapping this exact React app with **Capacitor** + native Kotlin/Swift services):\n1. **3x Shake while locked:** An Android `ForegroundService` reading `SensorManager` (`TYPE_ACCELEROMETER`) with a `PARTIAL_WAKE_LOCK`.\n2. **Secret Voice Code while locked:** An on-device wake-word listener (e.g., Porcupine / Android `VoiceInteractionService`) running in a foreground service.\n3. **Screen Tap / Hardware Button while locked:** An Android `AccessibilityService` (or Power/Volume button rapid-press trigger) + `showWhenLocked` emergency screen + `CALL_PHONE` permission for true zero-tap auto-calling.\n\n*(Note: iOS is even stricter than Android and does not allow third-party apps to listen to screen taps or custom wake words while locked\342\200\224Android supports all three via native background services.)*\n\nIf you\'d like, I can generate the ready-to-use **Capacitor + Android Kotlin native service code** for these 3 real lock-screen triggers so you can plug this frontend straight into an Android APK later."
    thinking: "**Clarifying the Query**\n\nI\'m focused on directly addressing the user\'s question about lock screens. My goal is to be transparent and straightforward. I\'m prioritizing clarity and practicality in my response, avoiding any unnecessary padding. So, I will be clearly answering what the lock screen actually refers to.\n\n\n**Defining Current Functionality**\n\nI\'m now detailing the current functionality within the web application context. Specifically, the \"Lock-Screen Shield\" operates as an app-level standby lock screen, functioning inside the browser environment and NOT the system lock screen. The reason for this limitation is the OS security restrictions on web applications accessing device features like the microphone, accelerometer, and screen taps. I\'m focusing on clarifying how the \"Lock-Screen Shield\" works when active, utilizing the Screen Wake Lock API and outlining the available interactive features.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "c-YCa_jEJtjQz7IPu4jm6Qk"
    modified_response: "**Honest answer: Right now, it works only inside the app (as an in-app Lock-Screen Standby mode), NOT on your phone\'s real system lock screen.**\n\n### Why?\nBecause this version is a **Web Application (running in a browser like Chrome/Safari)**. Both **Android and iOS strictly block websites** from reading the accelerometer (shake), listening to the microphone (voice code), detecting screen taps, or placing automatic phone calls once you press the phone\'s power button to lock the device or close the browser.\n\n### What works right now in this Web App\n- When you tap **Lock Shield**, OurSafe uses the browser\'s **Screen Wake Lock** to keep the screen from sleeping and displays a dark lock-screen clock cover.\n- As long as that shield screen is open, **3x Shake**, **Secret Voice Code**, and **Double/Triple Screen Tap** all work live without navigating inside the app dashboard.\n\n### What is needed for the *real* total phone lock screen (when phone is locked / app closed)\nTo work on the actual OS lock screen (like **\"Hey Google\"** or Android\'s built-in Emergency SOS), this frontend needs to be wrapped or built as a **Native Android/iOS app** (for example, wrapping this exact React app with **Capacitor** + native Kotlin/Swift services):\n1. **3x Shake while locked:** An Android `ForegroundService` reading `SensorManager` (`TYPE_ACCELEROMETER`) with a `PARTIAL_WAKE_LOCK`.\n2. **Secret Voice Code while locked:** An on-device wake-word listener (e.g., Porcupine / Android `VoiceInteractionService`) running in a foreground service.\n3. **Screen Tap / Hardware Button while locked:** An Android `AccessibilityService` (or Power/Volume button rapid-press trigger) + `showWhenLocked` emergency screen + `CALL_PHONE` permission for true zero-tap auto-calling.\n\n*(Note: iOS is even stricter than Android and does not allow third-party apps to listen to screen taps or custom wake words while locked\342\200\224Android supports all three via native background services.)*\n\nIf you\'d like, I can generate the ready-to-use **Capacitor + Android Kotlin native service code** for these 3 real lock-screen triggers so you can plug this frontend straight into an Android APK later."
