  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Intent**\n\nI see the user wants the lock screen and home screen targeted. I am considering that this is the main, if not the only, goal here. Therefore, I need to focus on honesty and helpfulness as primary goals. I am reminded of the fundamentals of software.\n\n\n**Clarifying Native Integration**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the realistic capabilities. It\'s clear that JavaScript within a browser preview can\'t directly manipulate the Android lock screen. However, I\'m envisioning a robust Android native integration package using Capacitor and Kotlin. This involves a foreground service, overlay, accessibility/gesture services, wake-word, boot receiver, and a clean native bridge to differentiate between the Android package and the web version, focusing on providing the features.\n\n\n**Envisioning Native Features**\n\nI\'m now concretely defining the native Android integration. The \"Lock Shield\" functionality will directly target the phone\'s *actual* lock screen using `FLAG_SHOW_WHEN_LOCKED` and a foreground service for shake detection and speech recognition. Simultaneously, I\'m integrating a persistent overlay for home screen protection, alongside a handy SOS widget. I\'m upgrading the web app, allowing seamless transition to a phone main lock screen view.\n\n\n**Defining Native Implementation**\n\nI\'m solidifying the Android APK implementation. It\'s becoming clearer. The user interface will encompass: a lock screen view mirroring a real phone\'s, a home screen view with our \"OurSafe Shield Bubble\" and SOS widget, and an Android setup panel. This panel will display the bridge status between web and native services, syncing preferences directly using `syncConfig` for consistent behavior across builds, which is a key priority. We need no modification for `install_applet_package`.\n\n\n**Refining Capacitor Implementation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on keeping the web app lean. I can cleanly type the Capacitor bridge with TypeScript and keep the web build fast. Plus, I\'m providing ready-to-use Android Kotlin files and `capacitor.config.ts`. I also need to make sure we make a PWA manifest and meta tags so users can add the app directly to their home screens, with a direct lock shield shortcut. Looks like I should review the `pwa-integration` skill, too.\n\n\n"
