  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Solution**\n\nOkay, I\'m now zeroing in on a dual-layer approach: a web-based component and an installable home screen application. My current focus is on a complete solution, where there are no broken promises and absolutely no missing pieces. The goal is to give the user everything they need.\n\n\n**Developing Native Capabilities**\n\nI\'ve made significant progress by focusing on the Android native side, specifically within the `/android-native/` directory. I\'m providing complete Kotlin files, manifest permissions, and a Capacitor plugin bridge, ensuring a ready-to-compile solution after `npx cap add android`. The core of this is the `OurSafeBackgroundShieldService.kt`, an Android `ForegroundService` employing `PARTIAL_WAKE_LOCK`. This includes real shake detection via `SensorManager` and a always-on \"secret voice code\" listener using `SpeechRecognizer`.\n\n\n**Implementing Home Screen Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating critical features. A floating overlay, leveraging `WindowManager.LayoutParams.TYPE_APPLICATION_OVERLAY`, will shield both the lock and home screens. Rapid tap detection via an `AccessibilityService` is being implemented. I\'m also developing a home screen widget and a Capacitor plugin to link our React app to the native background service. This allows SOS actions, including GPS SMS and calls, to be initiated directly from the home screen.\n\n\n**Defining Permissions and Bridge**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the Android manifest, ensuring all necessary permissions are declared, including `FOREGROUND_SERVICE`, `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW`, `CALL_PHONE`, and others. A TypeScript bridge (`/src/utils/nativeShieldBridge.ts`) automatically handles synchronization. It directly starts and syncs the service on the phone\'s real main lock and home screens. It also requests system permissions and handles native trigger events. I\'ve also incorporated fallback for the web browser context.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "-OcCa5ejNt66qtsPtM3S4A8"
