    thinking: "**Achieving Total Clarity**\n\nI\'ve achieved total clarity! Every file I examined has illuminated the user\'s requirements. I\'ve successfully mapped their requests to tangible improvements. The implications are now clear to me, leading to a definitive path forward.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Representation**\n\nI\'m now honing in on the lock screen visualization. The current implementation, as evidenced in `EmergencyLockScreenShield.tsx` and `OurSafeBackgroundShieldService.kt`, is far too intrusive. My focus is on a refined presentation: a clean, familiar lock screen where \"OurSafe\" is represented minimally, perhaps as a discrete SOS icon or a compact label and icon. I envision this as a configurable option in the Settings, with a collapsible \"Controls\" button remaining available.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Safety Systems**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the safety systems\' default states and user controls. My plan is to ensure all four methods\342\200\224shake, code, tap, and wake word\342\200\224are active by default, enhancing immediate usability. A centralized \"Always-On Safety Systems\" control panel will be essential in Settings, with a master switch and individual toggles for each feature. The aim is to balance immediate security with user configurability.\n\n\n**Defining User Control**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the user\'s interaction with the system, focusing on controls in Settings for the Safety Systems. The plan: ensure all four SOS methods are enabled by default (shake, voice, tap, wake word) and centrally managed via an \"Always-On Safety Systems\" control panel with a master switch and individual toggles. The voice-controlled mode will be implemented for full app control.\n\n\n**Implementing Wake Word Integration**\n\nI\'m excited about developing an \"OurSafe\" wake word engine akin to \"Hey Google.\" The always-on listener will simultaneously monitor for emergency secret codes (\"Red Rose,\" etc.) to trigger SOS, as well as the app wake word (\"OurSafe,\" etc.). Upon hearing the wake word, the system will instantly wake OurSafe, unlock the lock screen, and connect the Gemini Live Voice Controller, enabling full app control, including immediate execution of spoken commands.\n\n\n**Expanding Voice Control**\n\nI\'m now integrating seamless voice control. I\'m focusing on offline, instantaneous functionality for key commands like \"OurSafe open travel mode\" and online, full Gemini AI Live Voice capabilities. Android Native (`OurSafeBackgroundShieldService.kt`) will detect \"OurSafe\" in the background, even when the app is closed, launching `MainActivity` for full voice control. A persistent Floating Orb will enable voice control from any screen with a tap or voice command.\n\n\n**Reinforcing Emergency Triggers**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on reinforcing the SOS triggers. My plan ensures the three methods (shake, code, tap) function consistently. Each trigger will fetch fresh GPS data, initiate an emergency call, and share the live location with guardians and the police via API and activate the live location sharing session. I\'m also ensuring that in `OurSafeBackgroundShieldService.kt`, automatic GPS SMS and calls are sent even when the phone is locked.\n\n\n\n**Enhancing Visual Aesthetic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a new background for the app. The current solid color feels uninspired, so I plan to implement a calm botanical ambient canvas background. This design will incorporate subtle sage/olive radial light blooms and a fine botanical grid texture. This will be fixed to the viewport for smooth, clean card layouts and better visual depth. On the Home page, I\'ll streamline the \"Community Reviews\" section and add a status strip for the four hands-free entryways for quick access to Lock Shield and Settings. I also plan to re-evaluate the background in `EmergencyLockScreenShield.tsx`.\n\n\n"
