  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Batch Execution**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a clean execution plan for file updates, aiming for logical parallel batches. I\'ve begun defining the first batch, which centers around updating types, defaults, and the CSS canvas background. Specifically, this initial batch will add to `/src/types/safety.ts`.\n\n\n**Adding User Preferences**\n\nI\'m now integrating user preferences to enhance the OurSafe application\'s functionality. My plan involves adding new settings like the wake word toggle and phrase, lock screen display style, and a master always-on safety switch. I\'m focusing on ensuring these preferences are stored correctly in `/src/utils/storage.ts` with sensible defaults. I\'m also ensuring the native Android service in `/src/utils/nativeShieldBridge.ts` syncs cleanly with these settings. Finally, I\'m adding CSS classes in `/src/index.css` for a custom canvas background and lock screen wallpaper.\n\n\n**Implementing Wake Word Detection**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing the wake word feature and its integration with emergency triggers. This involves making the application always-listening for a user-defined wake word phrase. I\'m focusing on the implementation details within `/src/hooks/useEmergencyQuickTriggers.ts`, ensuring the application respects the master safety switch. This covers how the wake word is recognized, how to extract a command, and how to play a soft chime upon detection. I\'ve also added a test function.\n\n\n**Designing Lock Screen Shield**\n\nI\'m now completely redesigning the lock screen shield, aiming for a more discreet and practical approach. The new `/src/components/EmergencyLockScreenShield.tsx` will no longer be a full-screen takeover. Instead, it will mimic a realistic phone lock screen, featuring a clock, date, and wallpaper. A floating SOS icon, whose actions are customizable by user preference, will be the sole interactive element. My focus is on making this as unobtrusive as possible while maintaining immediate emergency access through multi-touch, voice, or shake inputs.\n\n\n"
